<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">A <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-star-forest is a forest with at most <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> connected components where each component is a star. Let <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimum integer such that the complete graph on <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices can be decomposed into <math id="abstract1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="abstract1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-star-forests. Pach, Saghafian and Schnider showed that <math id="abstract1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}(n)=\lceil 3n/4\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}(n)=\lceil 3n/4\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>. In this paper, we show that <math id="abstract1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{3}(n)=5n/9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{3}(n)=5n/9</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="abstract1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of 27. Further, for <math id="abstract1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, we show that <math id="abstract1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k}(n)=n/2+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}(n)=n/2+2</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="abstract1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;2k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="abstract1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\equiv 4\pmod{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\equiv 4\pmod{12}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our results disprove a conjecture of Pach, Saghafian and Schnider.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is a classic theme in combinatorics to determine the minimum number of subgraphs of a certain type that a graph <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed into. Here a decomposition of a graph <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a family of subgraphs that partitions <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. For example, the seminal result of Graham and Pollak<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> shows that <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the complete graph on <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices, cannot be decomposed into less than <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> complete bipartite graphs (on the other hand, it is easy to see that <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed into <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> stars). Other known results under this theme have also been obtained by Vizing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> for matchings, by Lovász <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> for paths and cycles, and by Nash-Williams <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> for forests.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">star-forest</span> is a forest whose components are all stars. Akiyama and Kano <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> proved that <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be decomposed into less than <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil n/2\rceil+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil n/2\rceil+1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests and this bound is tight.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For integer <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, a <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-star-forest</span> is a star-forest with at most <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> connected components. Recently, Pach, Saghafian and Schnider <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> studied the decomposition of cliques into <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-star-forests. Let <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> be the minimum integer such that the complete graph on <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> vertices can be decomposed into <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="S1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-star-forests. Pach, Saghafian and Schinider proved the following theorem.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, Theorem 4)<span id="S1.Thmthm1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{2}(n)=\lceil 3n/4\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{2}(n)=\lceil 3n/4\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We use the notation <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(v;u_{1},\dots,u_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(v;u_{1},\dots,u_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> to denote the star with center <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> and leaves <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1},\dots,u_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1},\dots,u_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The construction for <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1 ([], Theorem 4). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> can be described as follows. For simplicity, we will only describe it for even integer <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2t</annotation></semantics></math> and label the vertices as <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{1},\dots,v_{2t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{1},\dots,v_{2t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (indices are modulo <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2t</annotation></semantics></math>). For <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq t</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the 2-star-forest consisting of stars <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(v_{i};v_{i+1},\dots,v_{i+t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(v_{i};v_{i+1},\dots,v_{i+t-1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S(v_{i+t};v_{i+t+1},\dots,v_{i+2t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S(v_{i+t};v_{i+t+1},\dots,v_{i+2t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>. One can check that <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1},\dots,S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1},\dots,S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> cover all edges but those of the form <math id="S1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}v_{i+t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}v_{i+t}</annotation></semantics></math>. This construction, called <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">broken double star</span> in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, shows that <math id="S1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be decomposed into at most <math id="S1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/2+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/2+1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests. Further, Antić, Glišić and Milivojčević <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> showed that such decomposition is unique up to isomorphism. This result will be useful later in this paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, Theorem 1)<span id="S1.Thmthm2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2t</annotation></semantics></math> be an even integer. Then any decomposition of <math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> into
<math id="S1.Thmthm2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t+1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests is a broken double star decomposition.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that for 2-star-forests, one can decompose the edges of the form <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}v_{i+t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}v_{i+t}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil n/4\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil n/4\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> matchings of size at most 2, which provides the construction for <a href="#S1.Thmthm1" title="Theorem 1.1 ([], Theorem 4). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>. Similarly, if the goal is to decompose <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-star-forests, then one can make use of <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1},\dots,S_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1},\dots,S_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> plus <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lceil\frac{n}{2k}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lceil\frac{n}{2k}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> matchings of size at most <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>. Pach, Saghafian and Schider conjectured that this construction is best possible.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conj">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmthm3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k}(n)\geq\left\lceil\frac{(k+1)n}{2k}\right\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mo>⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}(n)\geq\left\lceil\frac{(k+1)n}{2k}\right\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we determine the value of <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> and infinitely many integers <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, which disproves <a href="#S1.Thmthm3" title="Conjecture 1.3 ([]). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Conjecture</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{3}(n)=5n/9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{3}(n)=5n/9</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> when <math id="S1.Thmthm4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is positive and a multiple of 27.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_thm">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmthm5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmthm5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k}(n)=\frac{n}{2}+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}(n)=\frac{n}{2}+2</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;2k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmthm5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\equiv 4\pmod{12}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\equiv 4\pmod{12}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In fact, by <a href="#S1.Thmthm2" title="Theorem 1.2 ([], Theorem 1). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.2</span></a> we know that <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{k}(n)\geq\frac{n}{2}+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{k}(n)\geq\frac{n}{2}+2</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;2k</annotation></semantics></math>, and it turns out that this lower bound is tight for <math id="S1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=12m+4(m\geq 1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=12m+4(m\geq 1)</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall the definition of <math id="S1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-star-forest, this actually proves <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> for <math id="S1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The rest of this paper is structured as follows. Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Decomposition into 3-star forests ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> consists of three subsections where in Subsection <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Lower bounds ‣ 2 Decomposition into 3-star forests ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> we prove the lower bound for <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a> and in Subsections <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 The construction for 𝑛=27 ‣ 2 Decomposition into 3-star forests ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> and  <a href="#S2.SS3" title="2.3 Blowup ‣ 2 Decomposition into 3-star forests ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> we present constructions that match the lower bound. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Decompostion into 4-star forests ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm5" title="Theorem 1.5. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.5</span></a> for <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=4</annotation></semantics></math> by first presenting a construction for <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=16</annotation></semantics></math> in Subsection <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 The construction for 𝑛=16 ‣ 3 Decompostion into 4-star forests ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, and then extend it to <math id="S1.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=12m+4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=12m+4</annotation></semantics></math> in the rest of Sections <a href="#S3" title="3 Decompostion into 4-star forests ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Decomposition into 3-star forests</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we prove Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmthm4" title="Theorem 1.4. ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefi1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_defi">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefi1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefi1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefi1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefi1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefi1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmdefi1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be a collection of star-forests. The <span id="Thmdefi1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">root-hypergraph</span> <math id="Thmdefi1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmdefi1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as follows: every star-forest <math id="Thmdefi1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> in the decomposition corresponds to a unique hyperedge <math id="Thmdefi1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmdefi1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>, such that vertices in <math id="Thmdefi1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{S}</annotation></semantics></math> are exactly centers of the stars in <math id="Thmdefi1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For example, the root-hypergraph of the broken double star described earlier has edges <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i},v_{i+t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i},v_{i+t}\}</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq t</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{1},~v_{2},~\dots,~v_{t}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{1},~v_{2},~\dots,~v_{t}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This concept is useful in our following proofs, and was used by Pach, etc. in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> to prove their result for 2-star-forests (Theorem 4), although they did not name it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be a collection of star-forests decomposing <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> be its root-hypergraph. If <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{C}|&lt;n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{C}|&lt;n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.Thmthm1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> has no isolated vertex.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Suppose there exists a vertex <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> not contained in any hyperedges in <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. By definition it means that <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> is not a center for any star-forests in <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that each star-forest contain at most one edge containing <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, to cover all <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> edges containing <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, there are at least <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> star-forests in <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Lower bounds</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we show that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{3}(n)\geq\frac{5n}{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>9</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{3}(n)\geq\frac{5n}{9}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Suppose (for contradiction that) there exists a minimal <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{3}(n)&lt;\frac{5n}{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mn>9</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{3}(n)&lt;\frac{5n}{9}</annotation></semantics></math>, giving rise to a decomposition of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{3}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{3}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> 3-star-forests. Let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding collection of 3-star-forests and let <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> be its root-hypergraph. Note that <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{C}_{0}|&lt;5n/9&lt;n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{C}_{0}|&lt;5n/9&lt;n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, so by <a href="#S2.Thmthm1" title="Proposition 2.1. ‣ 2 Decomposition into 3-star forests ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has no isolated vertex. Moreover, one can show that every hyperedge in <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> contains at least two vertices. Indeed, if <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> contains a 1-star-forest <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, then by deleting the center of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> itself from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{3}(n-1)\leq F_{3}(n)-1&lt;\frac{5(n-1)}{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>9</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{3}(n-1)\leq F_{3}(n)-1&lt;\frac{5(n-1)}{9}</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting with the minimality of <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=F_{3}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=F_{3}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of hyperedges in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of size 2. In other words, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>-star-forests in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus there are <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(m-r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(m-r)</annotation></semantics></math> hyperedges in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> of size 3, and we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{P\in V(B_{0})}deg_{B_{0}}(P)=\sum_{e\in B_{0}}|e|=2r+3(m-r)=3m-r," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{P\in V(B_{0})}deg_{B_{0}}(P)=\sum_{e\in B_{0}}|e|=2r+3(m-r)=3m-r,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="deg_{B_{0}}(P)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">deg_{B_{0}}(P)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the degree of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e. the number of hyperedges in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> has no isolated vertices, every vertex has degree <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us focus on vertices with degree 1 in <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and denote the set of these vertices by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, the set of other vertices by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\geq 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\geq 2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmthm2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmthm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmthm2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">No hyperedge in </span><math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> contain two vertices in </span><math id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For every vertex of degree 2 in </span><math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, the two hyperedges containing it will not both contain some vertex in </span><math id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If there exists a hyperedge <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q\in V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q\in V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the star-forest corresponding to <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> doesn’t cover the edge <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ</annotation></semantics></math>, since <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> are both centers in that star-forest. Further, since <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q\in V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q\in V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, no other star-forests use <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> as center, thus <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ</annotation></semantics></math> is also not covered by any other star-forests. This contradicts with <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> decomposing <math id="S2.I2.ix1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose that a degree 2 vertex <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> is connected to <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1},Q_{2}\in V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1},Q_{2}\in V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> by 2 hyperedges in <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively, and that <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is center of the star containing <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in the decomposition, without loss of generality. Then the star-forest corresponding to the hyperedge containing <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot contain edge <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Further, the star-forest corresponding to the hyperedge connecting <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot contain edge <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> either. Thus <math id="S2.I2.ix2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is not covered by any star-forests, a contradiction.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of degree-<math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> vertices in <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|V_{1}|=p_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|V_{1}|=p_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Calculate the number and degree sum of vertices we have:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j\geqslant 1}p_{j}=n;" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j\geqslant 1}p_{j}=n;</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j\geqslant 1}j\cdot p_{j}=3m-r." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j\geqslant 1}j\cdot p_{j}=3m-r.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Furthermore, consider the bipartite graph <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> between <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\geq 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\geq 2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in V_{\geq 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in V_{\geq 2}</annotation></semantics></math>, edge <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="PQ" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">PQ</annotation></semantics></math> is in <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P,Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P,Q</annotation></semantics></math> is connected by a hyperedge in <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <a href="#S2.F1" title="In 2.1 Lower bounds ‣ 2 Decomposition into 3-star forests ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for an example). By <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.1 Lower bounds ‣ 2 Decomposition into 3-star forests ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>(1), every vertex <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\in V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\in V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> has degree 1 or 2 in <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is determined by the size of the hyperedge connecting <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus the number of edges in <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r+2(p_{1}-r)=2p_{1}-r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r+2(p_{1}-r)=2p_{1}-r</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the degree of every vertex <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q\in V_{\geq 2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q\in V_{\geq 2}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is controlled by its degree in <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="deg_{B_{1}}(Q)\leq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">deg_{B_{1}}(Q)\leq j</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="deg_{B_{0}}(Q)=j\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">deg_{B_{0}}(Q)=j\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="deg_{B_{1}}(Q)\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">deg_{B_{1}}(Q)\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="deg_{B_{0}}(Q)=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">deg_{B_{0}}(Q)=2</annotation></semantics></math> by <a href="#S2.Thmthm2" title="Lemma 2.2. ‣ 2.1 Lower bounds ‣ 2 Decomposition into 3-star forests ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>(2). Thus by double counting the number of edges in <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2p_{1}-r\leqslant p_{2}+\sum_{j\geqslant 3}j\cdot p_{j}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>⩽</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>⩾</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></munder><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2p_{1}-r\leqslant p_{2}+\sum_{j\geqslant 3}j\cdot p_{j}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
