<p id="S3.SS5.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Finally, we add edges between <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{Y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{Y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{Z_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{Z_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> as shown in <a href="#S3.F6" title="In 3.5 Boundry Rule ‣ 3 Decompostion into 4-star forests ‣ Decomposition of Cliques into 𝑘-Star-Forests" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, actually exactly the same as what we have done to <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{16}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>16</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{16}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
